.obsidian/
__pycache__/
.venv/
*.pyc

# Toolkit-managed caches. _meta/.cache/ is written by the MCP framework itself:
# fts.db (an SQLite full-text index over every document) and siblings/ (cached
# sibling-corpus indexes for cross-corpus citation resolution). Neither is an
# input to anything — both rebuild on demand — and committing a binary SQLite
# file that changes on every ingest is exactly the kind of repository bloat that
# is painful to undo later.
_meta/.cache/

# corpus-detect-changes writes this to the repo root on every run.
changed-sources.tsv

# GitHub Pages build output, if this corpus publishes a site.
site/

# Secret-shaped files. Nothing here should ever produce one, which is precisely
# why these are worth listing: a public civic-data repo gets no second chance
# once a credential is pushed, and the cost of the safety net is three lines.
*.env
.env
*.pem
*.key
credentials.json

.DS_Store

# Source PDFs are NOT committed; frontmatter declares snapshot_policy: hash-only.
# 229 reports for 2016-2018 alone are 336 MB, and the full 789-source corpus would be
# over a gigabyte. The committed .txt is what source_sha256 hashes and what the
# full-text coverage check diffs against, so hash-only keeps both guarantees. What is
# lost is the archival copy -- a real cost here, since 41 manifest URLs already 404.
_meta/snapshots/*.pdf

# The semantic vector artifact. DERIVED and REBUILDABLE from the documents beside it
# (`python3 -m corpus_toolkit.semantic.build`), and large — a chunk-level index runs from a
# few MB to a few hundred. It is built on a GPU host and mounted into the container at
# runtime, never committed and never baked, the same arrangement the platform already uses
# for executive-regulatory-frameworks.
_meta/embeddings/
